(out/'learner-only.md').write_text('''# Make the milkshake slower?\n\nStatus: Draft.\n\nYou run a fast-food chain. Make your morning milkshake worth coming back for. First instinct: easier to sip, or longer to finish? No score.\n\nThe scene shows a full milkshake at the shop.\n\n- I make it thinner.\n- I make it thicker.\n\n## Second decision, after discovery\n\nNow change the wait, not the drink. These regulars know their order and want to get on the road. What do you give them?\n\nNext morning · imagined trial. Keep the thicker shake in both versions. Prepay skips the till, but locks the order before arrival. Pay at arrival keeps last-minute changes, but keeps the wait.\n\n- I add prepay and quick pickup.\n- I keep pay-and-change at arrival.\n''')
(out/'author-notes.md').write_text('''# Milkshake repair author notes\n\nStatus: Draft. Human newcomer tests: 0.\n\nLens: Toy followed by changed circumstance. Reference beat: one player control → visible state change, from the studio’s Toy card; compare desired slowness while consuming with unwanted slowness before consumption. This borrows the pattern, not a claim that this deck equals a full simulation.\n\nRole is chain operator; goal is a worthwhile morning purchase; controls alter the drink, then the pickup arrangement. Initial choice is unscored and made before customer evidence. Easier sipping and longer consumption both offer plausible benefits. Second choice protects either early departure or flexibility; for disclosed regulars who know their orders, prepay fits the stated goal. Failure path acknowledges the protected flexibility then supplies the worked commuter plan. No score anywhere. Two meaningful decisions; first consequence at one tap. Target roughly one minute, unmeasured.\n\nThe cup, till, shop, car and workplace remain in labelled before/after illustrations. These are schematic states, not measurements. Reduced motion loses no information. Approved embedded fox artwork is inherited unchanged from the template. No free-text input, no AI runtime, no simulated comprehension. Only deck artifacts, no app/prototype changes.\n\n## Source boundary\n\nEvidence supplied and checked by coordinator: HBS Online, https://online.hbs.edu/blog/post/jobs-to-be-done-examples; local source notes content-team/jtbd-premises/sources.md and food.md. Morning purchase timing, no other menu items, hunger/boredom/one-handed commute needs are reported case claims. Thicker consistency and prepaid checkout are recommendations, not established causal sales improvements. No sales figures or verified recipe timings are asserted.\n\nIllustrated cup depletion, positions, known-order regulars, prepay locking an order, and remaining till wait are authored simulation assumptions. The first illustration says it is not a measured recipe test. All second-move rules appear before commitment. The alternative breakfast-food category is a teaching comparison; no specific alternative’s performance is attributed to the source. There are no invented testimonials.\n\n## Honest weaknesses\n\nThe second move changes where time matters, not the customer’s entire circumstance. Its disclosed goal makes prepay a relatively easy choice. This is intentionally fair, but may still feel like feature matching: a cold fun gate must reject it if it does. The deck is a branching before/after storyboard, not a freely adjustable toy. Adding delight will not repair a failed hook. The concept names progress and circumstance, while comparison against other foods remains thin; a later chapter must not claim this opening alone teaches the full framework.\n\nNo browser check by this author; coordinator reserved browser review. Structural checker cannot establish visual fit, interest or comprehension. Next step: cold play review, then unfamiliar humans if it survives.\n''')
